Give the A3000 internal-SCSI driver the same chip-mem DMA bounce the A2091
card driver already has, so boot-time reads into high Fast-RAM buffers land.

amiga/alien/a3091.c drives the A3000 onboard "super-DMAC" + WD33C93 (autocon
0x0202F003 @ 0xDD0000).  Its startdma() programmed the caller's buffer address
straight into the DMAC's SAC register and fired the transfer with no bounce:

	device->sac = cp->addr + cp->nbyte - n;
	device->sdma = TRUE;

The super-DMAC cannot reliably reach an arbitrary 32-bit Fast-RAM address.  When
the buffer physical address falls outside what the DMAC can drive, the transfer
silently completes having moved nothing -- no bus error, no status bit, just
zeros left in the target buffer.  This is the "phantom-A3000 fastmem" hazard in
its DMA form: the register write succeeds but the data never lands.

It bites at boot.  getrdb() reads the Rigid Disk Block into a single static BSS
buffer (union block in sdpart.c).  While the buffer happened to sit at a low
enough address the DMA worked; once the kernel grew (~64 KB, from adding the
cdfs filesystem) the buffer moved up into a region the super-DMAC does not
reach, so getrdb() saw an all-zero block, found no RDSK, and sdopen() returned
ENXIO.  Root mount then failed:

	s5mountroot VOP_OPEN error 6
	PANIC: vfs_mountroot: cannot mount root: errno 30

A section-matched but code-inert cdfs stub (zero cdfs code, identical size)
reproduced the panic identically, proving the trigger is purely the buffer's
address, not any cdfs code.

The A2091 card driver (amiga/alien/a2091.c) never had this problem because its
startdma()/stopdma() already bounce through chip RAM for any buffer at or above
0x1000000: it AllocMem(MEMF_CHIP)s a scratch buffer, copies the caller's data
out before a write, points the DMAC at the chip buffer, and copies back in after
a read (freeing the buffer in stopdma).  Chip RAM is always DMA-reachable, so
the bounce is always safe.

This patch ports that bounce into a3091.c, adapted to its structures (global
`device`, `curunitp`, the local transfer count `n = tc0<<16|tc1<<8|tc2`, and
`cp->reading`/`cp->addr`/`cp->nbyte`).  Because a3091 keeps no per-card struct,
two file statics carry the in-flight allocation across the startdma->stopdma
lifecycle: `chipaddr` (the chip buffer, 0 when none) and `dma_tc` (its byte
count).  Copy directions mirror a2091 exactly:

  * write (cp->reading == FALSE): bcopy caller -> chip before starting DMA;
    the DMAC then reads out of chip RAM.
  * read  (cp->reading == TRUE): DMA fills the chip buffer, then stopdma
    bcopies chip -> caller once the transfer flushes.

The buffer is allocated in startdma and freed in stopdma on every path that
stops a DMA (completion, abort, disconnect/reselect), matching a2091's certified
lifecycle -- the same `unless (dma_on) return;` early-out still guards stopdma,
and the copy-back/FreeMem run only when `chipaddr` is non-zero, so the untouched
"no DMA started" and direct (low-address) paths free nothing.  The threshold is
a2091's proven `>= (caddr_t)0x1000000`; buffers below it still go direct.

Cost is a transient MEMF_CHIP allocation of exactly the transfer size for the
duration of each high-buffer DMA.  Boot getrdb() reads are a single RDB block
(tiny), so chip-mem pressure is negligible; if chip RAM were ever exhausted the
driver panics with a clear "a3091: no chip mem" rather than corrupting data --
same failure mode as a2091.

Apply against a stock Amix 2.1 source tree:
    cd /usr/sys && patch -p0 < a3091.c.patch      # target: amiga/alien/a3091.c

--- amiga/alien/a3091.c.orig	2026-07-09 23:31:35.614383950 +0300
+++ amiga/alien/a3091.c	2026-07-09 23:32:23.616349127 +0300
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
  */

 #include	"sys/types.h"
+#include	"memory.h"
 #include	"rico.h"
 #include	"sd.h"

@@ -143,6 +144,8 @@
 static uint		istate,
 			startcom	= 0x09;
 static bool		dma_on;
+static uint		*chipaddr,	/* chip-mem bounce buffer, 0 if none */
+			dma_tc;		/* byte count of the in-flight DMA */


 a3091queue( con, cp)
@@ -334,7 +337,16 @@
 		device->cntr = 1<<3 | 1<<2;
 	else
 		device->cntr = 1<<3 | 1<<2 | 1<<1;
-	device->sac = cp->addr + cp->nbyte - n;
+	dma_tc = n;
+	if (cp->addr >= (caddr_t)0x1000000) {
+		unless (chipaddr = AllocMem( n, MEMF_CHIP))
+			panic( "a3091: no chip mem");
+		unless (cp->reading)
+			bcopy( cp->addr+cp->nbyte-n, chipaddr, n);
+		device->sac = chipaddr;
+	}
+	else
+		device->sac = cp->addr + cp->nbyte - n;
 	device->sdma = TRUE;
 	dma_on = TRUE;
 }
@@ -352,6 +364,13 @@
 	device->cint = TRUE;
 	device->srst = TRUE;
 	dma_on = FALSE;
+	if (chipaddr) {
+		volatile struct sdcom	*cp = curunitp->comhead;
+		if (cp->reading)
+			bcopy( chipaddr, cp->addr+cp->nbyte-dma_tc, dma_tc);
+		FreeMem( chipaddr, dma_tc);
+		chipaddr = 0;
+	}
 }
